{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: forecasts are systematically too high versus realized prices (typically +1 to +6 EUR), especially from ~day 7 onward; apply a downward level shift to the whole path, larger at longer horizons.",
    "Trend/turning-point lag: when the market is in a sharp downtrend, the model follows too slowly and keeps prices elevated; increase responsiveness to recent negative momentum (e.g., amplify last 5–10 day slope).",
    "Insufficient downside tail: large drawdowns are underpredicted; add an extra downside adjustment when recent volatility is high and returns are negative (volatility-regime conditional correction).",
    "Over-optimistic mean reversion: the model expects rebounds that do not materialize (forecasts flatten/creep up while actual continues falling); dampen mean-reversion strength during sustained selloffs.",
    "Horizon-specific widening error: short horizon (days 1–5) errors are smaller; medium/long horizon (days 10–30) errors grow materially; use an increasing-with-horizon negative bias term (e.g., linear ramp)."
  ]
}
